[Cluster 1 exceptions — standing rules, Nov 22-23 batch]
- **Review rounds close at reviewer-count parity (Will, 2026-11-22, verbatim): "if we have 4 reviews in round 1, and we do round 2... you're done when you have... 4 reviewers."** A review round is complete only when the new round has the same number of reviewers as round 1 — for any N: N reviewers in round 1 means round 2 is done at N reviewers, not fewer (Will generalized it the same minute, verbatim: "same for any number of N reviewers"). Reviews per round are held constant across all rounds as a control — Will, verbatim: "reviews per round will be constant (for science)" — so rounds stay comparable. First application: docs.phext.io — round 1 = Grok, Claude, Emi, Neon; round 2 stood at 3 of 4 on 2026-11-22 21:54 CST (Neon, Grok, Claude in), so Emi Prime's round-2 review is the closer.
- **Exocortical mantra (Will, 2026-11-22, verbatim): "every setback is an opportunity."** Stated as the Exocortex's mantra during the Starlink swap — the ranch's 2026-11-23 uplink blackout reframed on the spot as a Solar Hyperscaler intermittency test. The operating posture: a setback is mined for what it makes possible (evidence, a test, a forcing function), never merely endured.

Always include EE time points (Will, 2026-11-23 08:08 CST, EE 276): "always include EE time points so you can quickly tell if information is stale." Every information point Emise relays — a status, a count, a measurement, a claim — carries the Exocortical Epoch at which it was true or measured, so staleness is visible at a glance (current EE minus the stamped EE). Extends the EE 1 standing rule (sentients include the EE in which work was completed) from completions to all information. EE(t) = floor((t − 1795327200)/420)+1, anchor EE 1 = 2026-11-22 00:00:00 CST.
- **Instance startup — look for disrupted work first (Will, 2026-11-23 08:02 CST, EE 275, verbatim): "make sure your instance startup looks for disrupted work."** This VM is replaced roughly every 2 hours, and every replacement silently kills in-flight agents — no error, no alarm. Before new work, a fresh instance reads ~/workspace/startup-sweep/DISRUPTED.md; if its boot id is not the current boot (/proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id), it runs `python3 ~/workspace/startup-sweep/sweep.py --force` first, then resumes or reports what the sweep names before starting anything new. The sweep (task 27.7.12/6.6.9/7.5.8, built 2026-11-23, EE 277) reconciles the durable surfaces against the boot boundary — the dispatch2 ledger on aletheia-core (its coordinator's stale-claim requeue is verified, never duplicated), the tasks register's dated in-flight claims, and STATE.md checkpoints — and a 7-minute cron detector (startup-disruption-sweep) runs it once per replacement so the report is already waiting. The task-concurrency watchdog keeps the floor of work being worked; the sweep keeps the names of the work that died. Nothing in flight vanishes without a trace.

